# Is the market fair?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Nozick: fair process makes a fair outcome Rawls: judge institutions from behind a veil of ignorance"
next: {"title":"What does perfect competition assume?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/30-what-does-perfect-competition-assume.cast"}

## Fair steps, unequal result
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - each fan
      - Wilt
    values:
      - [10, 10]
      - [10, 260]
    stage: 0
    value_labels: true
    icons:
      - ticket
      - basketball
    colors:
      - blue
      - orange
    y_label: $ thousand (illustrative)
    label_size: 26
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 280
    box:
      x: 70
      'y': 170
      w: 540
      h: 460
sources:
    - id: nozick1974
      title: Anarchy, State, and Utopia
      authors: Robert Nozick
      year: 1974
      finding: 'Wilt Chamberlain example (ch. 7): fans drop 25 cents of each admission price into a box with Chamberlain''s name; one million attend his home games in a season and he ends up with $250,000. Entitlement theory: justice in acquisition, transfer and rectification; historical, not patterned, principles; ''liberty upsets patterns''. ''Taxation of earnings from labor is on a par with forced labor'' (p. 169).'

    text tag_n "Nozick (host)" #2f6b8f x 810 y 625 font_size 32 hidden true
    text cl_n "fair steps → fair result" #2f6b8f x 810 y 585 font_size 28 hidden true
    text tag_r "Rawls (co-host)" #b5482e x 810 y 520 font_size 32 hidden true
    text cl_r "judge the rules" #b5482e x 810 y 480 font_size 28 hidden true
    text c25a "25¢ a fan" cites ["nozick1974"] x 810 y 360 font_size 32 hidden true
    text c25b "× 1,000,000 fans\n= $250,000" cites ["nozick1974"] x 810 y 270 font_size 32 hidden true
    text hist "history,\nnot pattern" #2f6b8f x 810 y 350 font_size 34 hidden true
    text quote "“Taxation of earnings\nfrom labor is on a par\nwith forced labor.”" cites ["nozick1974"] x 810 y 210 font_size 28 hidden true
    text cant "equal start\ncan't last" #b5482e x 810 y 220 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Is the market fair?"

Is the market fair? I (@draw tag_n@)'ll argue one answer, Robert Nozick's: if everyone starts fair a (@draw cl_n@)nd every trade is voluntary, whatever comes out is fair.

B: And I'l (@draw tag_r@)l argue John Rawls's: you can't judge a trade on its own. Yo (@draw cl_r@)u judge the rules of the whole game, as if you didn't know which player you'd be.

Nozick's own test case, from 1974. Start with (@draw bar_1@) any split yo (@draw bar_2@)u like; say everyone has the same, ten thousand dollars. Illustrative.
    draw axes

Now the basketball star Wilt (@highlight bar_2@) Chamberlain signs a deal: at every home game, each fan drops  (@draw c25a@)twenty-five cents into a box with his name on it.

B: And in one season, a million fans come.
    draw c25b

So each fan is down a quarter, and Wilt is up two hundred and fifty thousand dollars.
    animate stage 1 duration 5

    pause 0.3

B: An e (@highlight bar_1 bar_2@)qual town has become a very unequal one. Before we argue, you decide.

    quiz "Is the new, unequal split fair?" judge false voice b
        right: Hold on to your answer: we each argue a side.
        * Yes
        * No
        * It depends

    erase c25a c25b

Nozick says yes, it's fair. The start was fair, every fan  (@highlight bar_1@)paid of their own free will, and nobody else lost a cent.

That's his entitlement theory: what counts is how you (@draw hist@) came to hold something, by fair acquisition and fair transfer, not the pattern the holdings make.

B: Which is how he arr (@draw quote@)ives at his most famous line: taxing earnings is on a par with forced labour.

    erase quote

And his sharpest point: f (@highlight bar_1 bar_2@)ree choices upset any pattern. To keep the town equal, you'd hav (@draw cant@)e to stop people paying to watch Wilt.

B: Granted. Liberty upsets patterns. My o (@highlight tag_r cl_r@)bjection is to the start: nobody chose where they started.

    pause 0.5

## Behind the veil
vars:
    f:
      value: 0
      min: 0
      max: 25
      step: 0.5
    okA:
      expr: min(1,max(0,floor(11-f)))
    okB:
      expr: min(1,max(0,floor(23-f)))
    okC:
      expr: min(1,max(0,floor(26-f)))
    wA:
      expr: okA
    wB:
      expr: okB*(1-okA)
    wC:
      expr: okC*(1-okB)
sources:
    - id: rawls1971
      title: A Theory of Justice
      authors: John Rawls
      year: 1971
      finding: 'Principles of justice are those chosen in an original position behind a veil of ignorance; the difference principle: social and economic inequalities are to benefit the least advantaged (maximin reasoning).'
    - id: harsanyi1975
      title: Can the maximin principle serve as a basis for morality? A critique of John Rawls's theory
      authors: John C. Harsanyi
      year: 1975
      finding: An impartial chooser with an equal chance of being anyone maximises expected (average) utility, not the minimum; maximin is irrationally risk-averse.

    path base thick #3d3833 points [[60,210],[690,210]] hidden true
    shape glow #f2c14e shape rect x 160 y 400 width 200 height 400 bind.x 160*wA+370*wB+580*wC style.fill #f2c14e style.opacity 0.3 style.stroke_width 0 hidden true
    shape bA1 #b5482e shape rect x 100 y 222 width 50 height 24 bind {"style.opacity":"0.15+0.75*okA"} style.fill #b5482e style.opacity 0.9 hidden true
    text vA1 "10" x 100 y 254 font_size 24 hidden true
    shape bA2 #2f6b8f shape rect x 160 y 270 width 50 height 120 bind {"style.opacity":"0.15+0.75*okA"} style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.9 hidden true
    text vA2 "50" x 160 y 350 font_size 24 hidden true
    shape bA3 #2f6b8f shape rect x 220 y 390 width 50 height 360 bind {"style.opacity":"0.15+0.75*okA"} style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.9 hidden true
    text vA3 "150" x 220 y 590 font_size 24 hidden true
    text labA "A\nfree-for-all" x 160 y 165 font_size 24 hidden true
    group barsA members ["bA1","bA2","bA3"] hidden true
    group valsA members ["vA1","vA2","vA3"] hidden true
    group socA members ["barsA","valsA"] hidden true
    shape bB1 #b5482e shape rect x 310 y 236.4 width 50 height 52.8 bind {"style.opacity":"0.15+0.75*okB"} style.fill #b5482e style.opacity 0.9 hidden true
    text vB1 "22" x 310 y 282.8 font_size 24 hidden true
    shape bB2 #2f6b8f shape rect x 370 y 264 width 50 height 108 bind {"style.opacity":"0.15+0.75*okB"} style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.9 hidden true
    text vB2 "45" x 370 y 338 font_size 24 hidden true
    shape bB3 #2f6b8f shape rect x 430 y 342 width 50 height 264 bind {"style.opacity":"0.15+0.75*okB"} style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.9 hidden true
    text vB3 "110" x 430 y 494 font_size 24 hidden true
    text labB "B\nsafety net" x 370 y 165 font_size 24 hidden true
    group barsB members ["bB1","bB2","bB3"] hidden true
    group valsB members ["vB1","vB2","vB3"] hidden true
    group socB members ["barsB","valsB"] hidden true
    shape bC1 #b5482e shape rect x 520 y 240 width 50 height 60 bind {"style.opacity":"0.15+0.75*okC"} style.fill #b5482e style.opacity 0.9 hidden true
    text vC1 "25" x 520 y 290 font_size 24 hidden true
    shape bC2 #2f6b8f shape rect x 580 y 246 width 50 height 72 bind {"style.opacity":"0.15+0.75*okC"} style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.9 hidden true
    text vC2 "30" x 580 y 302 font_size 24 hidden true
    shape bC3 #2f6b8f shape rect x 640 y 258 width 50 height 96 bind {"style.opacity":"0.15+0.75*okC"} style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.9 hidden true
    text vC3 "40" x 640 y 326 font_size 24 hidden true
    text labC "C\ncompressed" x 580 y 165 font_size 24 hidden true
    group barsC members ["bC1","bC2","bC3"] hidden true
    group valsC members ["vC1","vC2","vC3"] hidden true
    group socC members ["barsC","valsC"] hidden true
    portrait rawls x 850 y 330 width 190 of "John Rawls" cameo true hidden true
    text veil "veil of ignorance" #3d3833 x 850 y 600 font_size 30 hidden true
    text veil2 "which bar is yours?" #8f887c x 850 y 560 font_size 28 hidden true
    text key "red = poorest third" #b5482e x 850 y 500 font_size 24 hidden true
    text dp "difference principle:\ninequality only if\nit lifts the bottom" #b5482e x 850 y 330 font_size 26 hidden true
    scratch avg work ["average:","A 70 · B 59 · C 32"] x 850 y 330 font_size 28 hidden true
    shape floorline #8a5fa8 shape rect x 375 y 210 width 640 height 3 bind.y 210+2.4*f style.fill #8a5fa8 style.opacity 0.9 hidden true
    text floorlab "floor" #8a5fa8 x 735 y 228 font_size 26 bind.y 210+18+2.4*f hidden true
    math rule tex "\\text{floor} = {f}" size 34 x 850 y 420 hidden true
    text cue_h "floor 0: Harsanyi" #2f6b8f x 850 y 340 font_size 26 hidden true
    text cue_r "floor 25: Rawls" #b5482e x 850 y 290 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Behind the veil"

B: Nozick (@draw base labA labB labC parallel true@) asks how you got what you hold. Rawls asks some (@draw rawls@)thing earlier: who wrote the rules that decided where you started?

B: His test: choose a society's (@draw veil@) rules behind a veil of ignorance. You don't know  (@draw veil2@)if you'll be rich or poor, talented or not, born here or there.

    erase rawls

B: So here are three societies, each split into a poorest, a middle and a richest third.  (@draw key@)The incomes are illustrative.
    draw barsA barsB barsC parallel true

B: (@draw valsA@) A is a free-for-all: ten, fifty, a hundred and fifty. B has a sa (@draw valsB@)fety net: twenty-two, forty-five, a hundred and ten. C is (@draw valsC@) compressed: twenty-five, thirty, forty.

    quiz "Behind the veil, which society do you pick?" judge false voice b
        intro: You'll land in one of those bars at random. Which society do you choose?
        right: Keep your pick, and see whose reasoning it matches.
        * A: free-for-all
        * B: safety net
        * C: compressed

B: Rawls chooses C. Behind the v (@highlight bA1 bB1 bC1@)eil, guard against the worst: pick the society whose p (@highlight socC effect box@)oorest do best.

But C isn't equal either.
    highlight bC1 bC3

B: Right, and that's his difference princi (@draw dp@)ple: inequality is just only if it makes the worst-off better off than they'd otherwise be.

    erase dp veil veil2 key

Nozick wouldn't play this game at all. But I'll push back the way the economist Joh (@draw avg_box avg_line_1@)n Harsanyi did: you're equally likely to be anyone, so pick the h (@draw avg_line_2@)ighest average. That's A, at seventy.

B: And take a one-in-thr (@highlight bA1 vA1@)ee chance of living on ten? Rawls thought nobody would gamble a whole life on that.

    erase avg

Then here's the dial between us: the floor  (@draw floorline floorlab rule@)you insist on. Societies whose poorest fall below it are out; of the rest, take (@draw glow@) the highest average.

At zer (@draw cue_h@)o it's Harsanyi, and A wins. Rais (@animate f 15 duration 2@)e the floor to fifteen and A is out: B wins. At twe (@animate f 25 duration 2@)nty-five, only C is left. That's  (@draw cue_r@)Rawls.

    animate f 0 duration 1.5

B: Try it: drag the floor and find exactly where A loses to B.
    explore params ["f"]

Just above ten. Anyone who wants any floor at all ab (@highlight bA1 vA1@)ove ten has already left Harsanyi.
    animate f 10.5 duration 2

    pause 0.5

## What the figure can settle
sources:
    - id: frohlich1987
      title: Laboratory Results on Rawls's Distributive Justice
      authors: Norman Frohlich, Joe A. Oppenheimer and Cheryl L. Eavey
      year: 1987
      finding: 'Groups choosing a distributive principle under imposed impartiality (a simulated veil) overwhelmingly chose maximising average income subject to a floor constraint; Rawls''s difference principle (maximin) was the least favoured. British Journal of Political Science 17(1): 1-21.'
      doi: 10.1017/S0007123400004580
    - id: milanovic2015
      title: 'Global Inequality of Opportunity: How Much of Our Income Is Determined by Where We Live?'
      authors: Branko Milanovic
      year: 2015
      finding: 'Two circumstances people do not choose, country of residence and the income distribution within that country, account for more than half of the variability of income across the world population. Review of Economics and Statistics 97(2): 452-460.'
    - id: nozick1974
      title: Anarchy, State, and Utopia
      authors: Robert Nozick
      year: 1974
      finding: 'On rectification (ch. 7): past injustices might be so great as to make necessary in the short run a more extensive state in order to rectify them.'

    text hn "Nozick" #2f6b8f x 245 y 645 font_size 52 hidden true
    text hr "Rawls" #b5482e x 755 y 645 font_size 52 hidden true
    text n1 "trade upsets patterns" x 245 y 565 font_size 40 hidden true
    text n1v "✓ consensus" #5a8a4a x 245 y 505 font_size 36 hidden true
    text n2 "holdings started fair" x 245 y 415 font_size 40 hidden true
    text n2v "conquest, slavery,\nseized land" #b5482e x 245 y 335 font_size 36 hidden true
    text r1 "people pick maximin" x 755 y 565 font_size 40 hidden true
    text r1v "lab: average + floor" #b5482e cites ["frohlich1987"] x 755 y 505 font_size 36 hidden true
    text r2 "start is largely luck" x 755 y 415 font_size 40 hidden true
    text r2v "✓ > ½ of income gaps:\ncountry you live in" #5a8a4a cites ["milanovic2015"] x 755 y 335 font_size 36 hidden true
    icon lab size 80 x 920 y 630 or ["microscope"] icon_look picture of "test tube" hidden true
    mark x_r1 around r1 #b5482e kind strike hidden true
    mark x_n2 around n2 #b5482e kind strike hidden true
    text verdict "which is fair?  contested" #b5482e x 500 y 235 font_size 44 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch m cites ["frohlich1987"] work [{"tex":"\\text{Rawls: } \\max\\ \\min(y_1, y_2, y_3)"},{"tex":"\\text{Harsanyi: } \\max\\ \\tfrac{1}{3}(y_1 + y_2 + y_3)"},{"tex":"\\text{Lab: } \\max\\ \\bar{y} \\ \\text{ with } \\min y_i \\ge \\text{floor}"}] x 500 y 420 font_size 30 hidden true
    path xy_base thick #3d3833 points [[200,300],[800,300]] hidden true
    shape qX0 #b5482e shape rect x 290 y 321.6 width 100 height 43.2 style.fill #b5482e style.opacity 0.9 hidden true
    text qX0v "12" x 290 y 367.2 font_size 34 hidden true
    text qX0l "poorest" #b5482e x 290 y 275 font_size 28 hidden true
    shape qX1 #2f6b8f shape rect x 410 y 444 width 100 height 288 style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.9 hidden true
    text qX1v "80" x 410 y 612 font_size 34 hidden true
    text qX1l "average" #2f6b8f x 410 y 275 font_size 28 hidden true
    text qXn "Society X" x 350 y 232 font_size 36 hidden true
    shape qY0 #b5482e shape rect x 590 y 332.4 width 100 height 64.8 style.fill #b5482e style.opacity 0.9 hidden true
    text qY0v "18" x 590 y 388.8 font_size 34 hidden true
    text qY0l "poorest" #b5482e x 590 y 275 font_size 28 hidden true
    shape qY1 #2f6b8f shape rect x 710 y 408 width 100 height 216 style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.9 hidden true
    text qY1v "60" x 710 y 540 font_size 34 hidden true
    text qY1l "average" #2f6b8f x 710 y 275 font_size 28 hidden true
    text qYn "Society Y" x 650 y 232 font_size 36 hidden true
    shape floor10 #8a5fa8 shape rect x 500 y 336 width 620 height 3 style.fill #8a5fa8 style.opacity 0.9 hidden true
    text floor10l "floor 10" #8a5fa8 x 880 y 336 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "What the figure can settle"

So the (@draw hn hr parallel true@) dial runs from Harsanyi's average to Rawls's floor. What do r (@draw r1@)eal people choose when you build them a veil?

B: Norman Frohlich, Joe Oppenheimer and Cheryl Eavey t (@draw lab@)ried it in 1987. Students chose a rule for paying their gro (@highlight r1@)up before they knew their own income.

Rawls's maximin (@draw x_r1@) came last. The clear favourite was in the mi (@draw r1v@)ddle: the highest average, with a floor.

    erase lab

B: Fair enough, people don't pick my rule. But they  (@highlight r1v@)didn't pick your pure average either. And what people choose isn't the same as what is just.

B: Now your side's problem. N (@draw n2@)ozick's test needs a fair start, and real holdings trace ba (@draw n2v@)ck through conquest, slavery and seized land.

He ad (@highlight hn@)mitted it: past injustice might call for a  (@draw x_n2@)bigger state, for a while, to put things right.

B: And luck is huge.  (@draw r2@)Branko Milanovic found that two things nobody chooses, your country and how it shares its income, account for more than (@draw r2v@) half of income differences across the world.

Nozick's r (@highlight hn@)eply: arbitrary or not, your talents are yours, and so is what others freely pay for them.

So how sure are we? That  (@draw n1@)free exchange upsets any pattern:  (@draw n1v@)consensus. Which test of fairness is right (@draw verdict@): contested. That's a question of values, and no figure settles it.

B: What the figure can do is make you say which (@highlight hn hr@) you believe: does history decide, or the rules you'd choose blind?

    pause 0.5

    hide hn hr n1 n1v n2 n2v x_n2 r1 r1v x_r1 r2 r2v verdict

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want the three rules as formulas?

@math

    erase mathq

Rawls: of everyone's income y, make the smallest as large as possible.
    draw m_box m_line_1

Harsanyi: make the average as large as possible.
    draw m_line_2

The lab's choice: the largest average, among the societies whose smallest y clears the floor.
    draw m_line_3

    erase m

@test

    erase mathq

    show hn hr n1 n1v n2 n2v x_n2 r1 r1v x_r1 r2 r2v verdict

    quiz "Land was seized from its owners a century ago, then sold on in fair, voluntary sales ever since. By Nozick's test, is today's owner's title just?" intro "Four new cases."
        right: No: the chain starts with a theft. Nozick's test runs back through the whole history, so rectification is owed.
        wrong: His test runs back to the start: here, no.
        * Yes: every recent sale was voluntary
        + No: the chain starts with a theft
        * Only if the poorest gain

    hide hn hr n1 n1v n2 n2v x_n2 r1 r1v x_r1 r2 r2v verdict

Two new societies, X and Y: their poorest and their average.
    group xyg members ["xy_base","qX0","qX0v","qX0l","qX1","qX1v","qX1l","qXn","qY0","qY0v","qY0l","qY1","qY1v","qY1l","qYn"]

    quiz "Society X: poorest 12, average 80. Society Y: poorest 18, average 60. Which does Rawls's maximin pick?" voice b
        right: Y: its poorest get 18 against 12; the averages don't count.
        wrong: Read only the poorest: here, Y.
        * X
        + Y
        * They tie

    draw floor10 floor10l

    quiz "Same two societies. A group insists nobody falls below 10, then maximises the average. X or Y?"
        right: X: both clear the floor of 10, and X has the higher average, 80.
        wrong: Floor first, then averages: here, X.
        + X
        * Y
        * Neither passes

    erase xyg floor10 floor10l

    show hn hr n1 n1v n2 n2v x_n2 r1 r1v x_r1 r2 r2v verdict

    quiz "Which of these could an experiment settle?" voice b
        right: What people choose behind a veil: that's behaviour, and a lab can measure it. Whether the choice is just is a question of values.
        wrong: A lab measures behaviour, not justice: here, the veil choice.
        * Whether Nozick is right
        + What people choose behind a veil
        * Whether talent is deserved

So: is the market fair? By Nozick's test it can be, when the start and every step are clean. By Rawls's, only if you'd choose its rules without knowing where you'd land.
    highlight hn hr

    pause 0.5

## Where next
